Introduction
I ran my liquidity-sweep scalper over eight months of USDCAD M1, from the first of January to early September. It closed 1,365 trades and gave back roughly two-thirds of a 10,000 USD account. The Strategy Tester summed it up in one line: profit factor 0.88, net loss 6,838.22 USD.
That line is true and almost useless. The EA trades two setups, a bullish order block after a swing low gets swept and the mirror image of it on the sell side. Same entry code, same risk model, same magic number, so as far as the tester is concerned it is one system. It isn't. When I finally split the trades by setup, 82% of the loss came from the bullish side alone. The bearish side lost money too, but less than a quarter as much. I had spent two weeks optimizing inputs that applied to both.
That is what this article fixes. CSetupLedger is a small class that keeps a separate record for every setup type in an EA. It rebuilds itself from the account's own deal history, so a restart or a deleted file doesn't wipe the record. Next to the raw win rate it prints a Wilson score interval, which answers the question I kept asking during testing: has this win rate earned any trust yet, or is it still three lucky trades? Then it compares that interval with the breakeven win rate implied by the setup's own wins and losses. How often a setup wins does not tell you, on its own, whether its trades were worth taking. What comes back is one of four short answers instead of a percentage you have to interpret: too few trades, undecided, trusted or rejected.Everything below comes out of code that runs, and the files are attached at the end of the article.
Why two setups drift apart
Take an EA with two entries. One buys the bullish sweep, one sells the bearish sweep. In the source file they look symmetrical, and in mine they are. The sell branch is the buy branch with the comparisons flipped.
The market has no reason to be. In a pair that spent the year drifting one way, one of those entries trades with the drift and the other leans against it. The spread is the same on both sides but the stops are not, so it eats a different share of each. That is a list of ways two mirror-image setups can end up with different results, not a claim about which one applies to mine. The account report never gets that far. It adds the two up and shows you one number that averages an edge with a leak, and describes neither.
The M15 run in the results section is exactly that situation: two setups, 131 trades, profit factor 1.00, net result 2.12 USD on a 10,000 USD account. Eight months of trading to move the balance by  0.02%. On its own I would file that under noise and move on. Split by setup it is +150.65 on one side and -148.53 on the other, and now there is something to work with.
Putting the setup Id in the magic number
Before anything can be scored, the trade record has to remember which setup opened it. There are two places to write that tag and only one of them survives.
The obvious one is the order comment. It's a string, you control it, and it shows up in the history. It is still the wrong choice. The documentation only calls it "order comment" and never promises to keep it intact, and in practice brokers overwrite it: a stop-out often comes back with so:, and plenty of brokers replace the whole comment with [sl] or [tp] when the stop or target fires. So the tag goes missing precisely on the trades that lost, which are the ones the statistics need most.
The magic number is the field MQL5 gives you for this job, and nothing in the broker's fill or stop-out handling rewrites it. That alone does not make it safe. Nothing stops a second EA, or an older version of this one, from using the same range and writing into the same record. What the field gives you is a place the broker leaves alone, not protection from your own bookkeeping.
The encoding is about as plain as it gets. One base value, one Id added on top:
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
//| MagicFor                                                         |
//| Magic number a setup trades under                                |
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
long
CSetupLedger::MagicFor(
const
int
setupId)
const
{
return
m_magicBase + (
long
)setupId;
  }
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
//| SetupFromMagic                                                   |
//| Decode a magic number back to a setup id. Returns -1 when the    |
//| magic does not belong to this EA.                                |
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
int
CSetupLedger::SetupFromMagic(
const
long
magic)
const
{
long
offset = magic - m_magicBase;
if
(offset <
0
|| offset >= (
long
)m_count)
return
-
1
;
return
(
int
)offset;
  }
In the scalper, whose magic base input is 552001, the bullish setup trades on 552001 and the bearish one on 552002. The demo EA does the same thing from a base of 772001, so the two can run on one account without ever being confused for each other. SetupFromMagic() returns -1 for anything outside the registered range, which is what keeps another EA's trades, or a trade you opened by hand, out of your numbers. Every read of the history goes through this function first.
Setups register themselves at startup and get their Id back:
ledger.Init(InpMagicBase,
_Symbol
);
SETUP_RSI_BOUNCE = ledger.Register(
"RSI Bounce"
);
SETUP_MA_CROSS   = ledger.Register(
"MA Cross"
);
SETUP_BREAKOUT   = ledger.Register(
"Breakout"
);
There is a consequence here that is easy to miss, and it is the one thing in this article that will hurt you if you get it wrong.
Registration order is a data format.
The Id lives nowhere except inside the magic numbers of trades that have already closed. Insert a new setup at position 1 next month and every old trade belonging to the previous setup 1 quietly becomes a trade of the new one. Nothing throws an error. The numbers are simply wrong from then on.
So new setups go at the end of the list, and if you retire a setup, leave its Register() call where it is so the Id stays taken. A three-line comment above the registration block explaining this earns its keep.
The base needs the same discipline one level up. Treat it as a namespace: one base per EA, a gap above it for the setups you have not written yet, and a new base when a rewrite changes what a setup actually means, so trades taken under the old definition are not folded into the new one. Init() also takes the chart symbol, and the rebuild skips history from anything else, which stops one base from mixing EURUSD trades into a USDCAD record. It cannot separate timeframes or EA versions for you. That part stays manual.
Writing the tag onto the trade is one line before the order goes out:
trade.SetExpertMagicNumber((
ulong
)ledger.MagicFor(setupId));
Reading the history correctly
This is where hand-rolled statistics code usually goes wrong. There are three traps and none of them announce themselves.
Trap 1: deals are not trades
MetaTrader 5 history is a list of deals, not a list of trades. A position closed in three parts produces three OUT deals. Loop over deals, count every profitable one as a win, and a single winning trade shows up as three wins. If your EA scales out, the win rate it prints is fiction. The entry side behaves the same way: an order filled in pieces leaves several IN deals behind, all carrying one position Id.
The unit of a trade is the position, keyed by DEAL_POSITION_ID, and every deal belonging to it has to be summed back into one result:
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
//| ProcessClosedPosition                                            |
//|                                                                  |
//| Score one closed position and fold it into the ledger. Every deal|
//| belonging to the position is summed, so a partially closed trade |
//| collapses back into a single result instead of counting as       |
//| several.                                                         |
//|                                                                  |
//| Pass setupId = -1 to recover the setup from the entry deal's     |
//| magic number. Returns false if the position is still open or does|
//| not belong to this EA.                                           |
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
