The closed flag matters more than it looks. Without it, a position that so far only has an entry deal would be scored the first time it is seen, as a loss of zero.
Trap 2: DEAL_PROFIT is not the result
DEAL_PROFIT is the raw price result. Swap and commission sit in separate fields. On a swing EA with wide targets the commission hardly moves the result, although the swap does. On a scalper whose target is a few pips, the commission can decide whether a trade won at all. A trade that made 3 USD of price movement and paid 4 USD in commission is a losing trade, and a ledger that scores it as a win is flattering you.
net above is profit plus swap plus commission. Commission already comes back negative, so it is added rather than subtracted.
Trap 3: POSITION_TICKET is not POSITION_IDENTIFIER
HistorySelectByPosition() expects a position identifier, and the identifier is what every deal carries in DEAL_POSITION_ID. POSITION_TICKET is a different property that usually holds the same number, which is why an earlier version of my scalper passed the ticket for months without anything looking broken. The documentation is specific about when the two come apart. The ticket is replaced when the server re-opens a position as part of a service operation, and charging swap is the most common one. On a netting reversal the ticket becomes that of the reversing order, while the identifier stays with the position.The identifier is the value the history is indexed by, so it is the one to pass.
There is no PositionSelectByIdentifier() in the language, so checking whether a tracked position is still open means walking the open list:
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
//| IsPositionOpenById                                               |
//| There is no PositionSelectByIdentifier(), so this walks the list.|
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
bool
IsPositionOpenById(
const
long
posId)
  {
for
(
int
i =
PositionsTotal
() -
1
; i >=
0
; i--)
     {
if
(
PositionGetTicket
(i) ==
0
)
continue
;
if
(
PositionGetInteger
(
POSITION_IDENTIFIER
) == posId)
return
true
;
     }
return
false
;
  }
It is O(open positions), and that is a handful of iterations. I would rather have the slow version that is right.
The account history is the database
The first version of this scorecard in the scalper was four global counters. They started at zero on every OnInit(). Restart the terminal, change one input, or let the EA reload after a weekend and the whole record was gone, and gone in a way nobody notices, because the panel just says 0/0 and you assume it is a fresh start.
Writing those counters to a file fixes the restart. It does not fix the file being deleted, the EA being moved to a second terminal, or a trade you closed by hand from your phone.
The account already stores every one of those events. So the ledger keeps no state that it cannot rebuild:
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
//| RebuildFromHistory                                               |
//|                                                                  |
//| Rebuild the whole ledger from the account's deal history.        |
//| Pass 1 finds every position this EA opened, via the entry deal's |
//| magic number, and keeps each position id once. A partially filled|
//| entry produces several DEAL_ENTRY_IN deals carrying the same     |
//| DEAL_POSITION_ID, and without that check the position would be   |
//| scored once per fill.                                            |
//| Pass 2 scores each of them.                                      |
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
void
CSetupLedger::RebuildFromHistory(
void
)
  {
   Reset();
if
(!
HistorySelect
(
0
,
TimeCurrent
()))
     {
Print
(
"CSetupLedger: HistorySelect() failed, ledger left empty."
);
return
;
     }
long
posIds[];
// position ids found in pass 1
int
posSetup[];
// setup id of each of them
int
n =
0
;
// number of positions found
//--- pass 1: collect the positions this EA opened
int
total =
HistoryDealsTotal
();
for
(
int
i =
0
; i < total; i++)
     {
ulong
d =
HistoryDealGetTicket
(i);
if
(d ==
0
)
continue
;
//--- only the entry deal tells us which setup opened this position
if
(
HistoryDealGetInteger
(d,
DEAL_ENTRY
) !=
DEAL_ENTRY_IN
)
continue
;
//--- trades this magic base made on another symbol are not ours
if
(m_symbol !=
""
&&
HistoryDealGetString
(d,
DEAL_SYMBOL
) != m_symbol)
continue
;
int
sid = SetupFromMagic(
HistoryDealGetInteger
(d,
DEAL_MAGIC
));
if
(sid <
0
)
continue
;
long
pid =
HistoryDealGetInteger
(d,
DEAL_POSITION_ID
);
if
(pid ==
0
)
continue
;
bool
known =
false
;
for
(
int
k =
0
; k < n && !known; k++)
if
(posIds[k] == pid)
            known =
true
;
if
(known)
continue
;
// another fill of an entry we already have
ArrayResize
(posIds,   n +
1
);
ArrayResize
(posSetup, n +
1
);
      posIds[n]   = pid;
      posSetup[n] = sid;
      n++;
     }
//--- pass 2: score each position
for
(
int
k =
0
; k < n; k++)
     {
double
net =
0.0
;
      ProcessClosedPosition(posIds[k], posSetup[k], net);
     }
//--- leave the global history selection in a sane state for the caller
HistorySelect
(
0
,
TimeCurrent
());
  }
Two filters sit in pass one and both earn their place. History from another symbol is dropped, because a magic base does not stop the same EA on a second chart from writing into the same account history. And a position Id is collected once, however many entry deals carry it. An order filled in three pieces leaves three IN deals behind, and without that check the position would be scored three times, which is trap 1 arriving from the entry side. The rebuild uses two passes because ProcessClosedPosition() calls HistorySelectByPosition().
That call replaces the global history selection, so iterating a full selection while calling it would pull the array out from under the loop. Pass one collects position IDs and setup IDs into local arrays, and pass two scores them. At the end the full selection is restored for whatever the caller does next.If you extend the class, keep that side effect in mind. It isn't a bug today because both callers expect it, but it is the kind of thing that breaks later in a way that takes an afternoon to find.
This runs in OnInit(), and once more in OnDeinit(). Nothing rebuilds while the EA is trading; a closed position is folded in one at a time, so the per-trade cost stays flat however long the account history gets.
The rebuild in OnDeinit() is there because of the tester. If a position is still open when a test ends, the tester closes it after the EA's last trade event. No handler ever scores that close, so without the rebuild the final block misses the tester's net by exactly that trade.
My first run of the demo EA did.
Why the raw win rate lies
Three wins out of three is a 100% win rate. It is also something a coin that lands heads 44% of the time manages about one attempt in twelve. Any statistic computed on a small sample is mostly noise, and a per-setup ledger creates small samples on purpose, by splitting one sample into several.
So next to the raw rate the class reports a Wilson score interval. The question it answers is a practical one: given this record, which true win rates are still consistent with it at 95% confidence? The lower bound is the pessimistic end of that range and the upper bound is the optimistic one, and both ends are useful. One stops you trusting a setup too early, the other tells you when the evidence has turned against it.
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
//| WilsonBound                                                      |
//|                                                                  |
//| Wilson score interval. Three wins out of three is a 100% raw win |
//| rate and means nothing. The bounds answer a better question:     |
//| which true win rates are still consistent with this record at the|
//| given confidence level? Small samples get a wide interval. Large |
//| samples get a narrow one.                                        |
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
double
CSetupLedger::WilsonBound(
const
int
setupId,
const
double
z,
const
bool
upper)
const
{
int
total = Total(setupId);
if
(total <=
0
)
return
upper ?
1.0
:
0.0
;
double
p  = (
double
)Wins(setupId) / (
double
)total;
// raw win rate
double
z2 = z * z;
double
centre = p + z2 / (
2.0
* total);
double
margin = z *
MathSqrt
(p * (
1.0
- p) / total + z2 / (
4.0
* total * total));
double
denom  =
1.0
+ z2 / total;
double
bound = (upper ? (centre + margin) : (centre - margin)) / denom;
//--- keep the bound inside 0..1
if
(bound <
0.0
) bound =
0.0
;
if
(bound >
1.0
) bound =
1.0
;
return
bound;
  }
WilsonLower() and WilsonUpper() are one-line wrappers around it. The z argument defaults to 1.96 in the class declaration, so the 95% case needs no extra parameter at the call site.
I used Wilson rather than the textbook normal approximation because the normal one falls apart at exactly the values that matter here. At 3 wins from 3 trades it produces an interval of zero width around 100%, an answer that is plainly nonsense. Wilson stays sensible at the extremes and at small counts, which is most of what a fresh ledger contains.
