Rows two and three have the same raw win rate and should not be read the same way. The width of the interval is what stops you. The first row is the clearest case: three wins from three trades leaves a range wide enough to include a coin that comes up heads 44% of the time, which is why the raw 100% deserves no weight at all.
Turning a percentage into a verdict
A win rate on its own is not a decision. 35% is excellent at 1:3 reward-to-risk and a slow death at 1:1. The breakeven rate falls straight out of the reward ratio:
//--- breakeven win rate for a nominal reward ratio
static
double
BreakevenWinRate(
const
double
rewardRatio)
  {
return
(rewardRatio <=
0.0
) ?
1.0
:
1.0
/ (
1.0
+ rewardRatio); }
At 1:2 that is 33.3%, at 1:1 it is 50%, at 1:3 it is 25%.
That formula was the first version of the test, and it has a hole in it. The ratio it uses is the one you asked for in the inputs, not the one the account paid out. A 2R target is not 2R once spread, slippage and commission have been taken off it, and a stop that is really hit at the worst price inside a spike is not 1R either. Judge a setup against the nominal ratio and you are grading it against a trade it never actually took.
The ledger already has the real version of that number, because it sums the winning nets and the losing nets separately as trades come in. Divide each by its count and you have the average win and the average loss the account really saw, commission and swap included:
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
//| RealisedReward                                                   |
//| Reward-to-risk as the closed trades actually came out, not the   |
//| ratio the EA aimed for. Returns 0 until the setup has at least   |
//| one win and one loss.                                            |
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
double
CSetupLedger::RealisedReward(
const
int
setupId)
const
{
double
avgLoss = AvgLoss(setupId);
if
(avgLoss <=
0.0
)
return
0.0
;
return
AvgWin(setupId) / avgLoss;
  }
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
//| BreakevenFor                                                     |
//|                                                                  |
//| The win rate this setup has to clear to break even. Once the     |
//| setup has both a win and a loss on record, the ratio of its own  |
//| averages is used, so spread, slippage and commission are already |
//| priced in. Before that there is nothing to measure, so the       |
//| nominal ratio from the EA inputs stands in.                      |
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
double
CSetupLedger::BreakevenFor(
const
int
setupId,
const
double
nominalRatio)
const
{
double
realised = RealisedReward(setupId);
if
(realised >
0.0
)
return
1.0
/ (
1.0
+ realised);
return
BreakevenWinRate(nominalRatio);
  }
Until a setup has both a win and a loss on record there is nothing to measure, so the nominal ratio stands in. After that the bar moves to whatever the setup is actually achieving. A setup running at 1:1.2 in practice needs 45.5% to break even, not the 33.3% its 1:2 input suggested.
The verdict then compares the interval against that bar, but only once the setup has enough closed trades behind it:
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
//| Verdict                                                          |
//|                                                                  |
//| Below the minimum sample there is no verdict at all. Above it:   |
//|   the whole interval above breakeven   -> trusted                |
//|   the whole interval below breakeven   -> rejected               |
//|   breakeven inside the interval        -> undecided              |
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
ENUM_SETUP_VERDICT CSetupLedger::Verdict(
const
int
setupId,
const
double
nominalRatio,
const
double
z)
const
{
int
total = Total(setupId);
if
(total <=
0
)
return
SETUP_NO_DATA;
if
(total < m_minTrades)
return
SETUP_TOO_FEW;
double
breakeven = BreakevenFor(setupId, nominalRatio);
if
(WilsonLower(setupId, z) > breakeven)
return
SETUP_TRUSTED;
if
(WilsonUpper(setupId, z) < breakeven)
return
SETUP_REJECTED;
return
SETUP_UNDECIDED;
  }
My first version had two answers, trusted and not trusted, and the second one covered too much. Twenty trades that have not proved anything yet, several hundred trades that still sit on the fence, and several hundred trades that have proved the setup does not clear its own bar all printed the same words, and they call for different reactions. They now read as too few trades, undecided and rejected. IsTrusted() is still there and still returns a boolean, because an EA gating entries only cares about one of the answers.
The minimum is 30 closed trades unless you change it with SetMinTrades(). I added it after reading my own logs. Without it, the M15 run marked the bullish setup trusted after two trades, both winners, because the Wilson lower bound for two wins from two is 34.2% and the bar before the first loss is the nominal 33.3%. Once a setup is past the minimum, almost everything reads undecided for a long time, which is the right answer, and it annoyed me for about a week before I accepted it.
The minimum only deals with tiny samples, not with streaks. In all five M1 runs the bearish setup read trusted on 23 January, at 16 wins from 31 trades, and the loss that followed took it straight back to undecided. In two of the runs it happened again four days later, at 20 wins from 41. By the end of the test the same setup had lost between 991 and 1,346 USD, depending on the run. If you check the verdict after every close,a short winning streak can push a marginal setup over the line for a trade or two. The bound makes that harder, not impossible, and an EA that acts on the verdict should use a stricter z or only look at fixed checkpoints.
One compromise is buried in Record(): a net result of exactly zero counts as a loss, because the test is net > 0.0. That is a judgement call. Scratch trades are rare, and if I am going to be wrong about them I would rather be wrong in the pessimistic direction.
Wiring it into an EA
The demo EA registers three setups that have nothing to do with each other: an RSI bounce out of oversold, an EMA cross in both directions, and a close outside the last N bars. The entry rules are plain on purpose. They exist to produce three different streams of trades, not to make money.
The ledger part of OnInit() is six lines. The second argument to Init() is the chart symbol, which is what the history filter above compares against:
ledger.Init(InpMagicBase,
_Symbol
);
SETUP_RSI_BOUNCE = ledger.Register(
"RSI Bounce"
);
SETUP_MA_CROSS   = ledger.Register(
"MA Cross"
);
SETUP_BREAKOUT   = ledger.Register(
"Breakout"
);
//--- the account history is the record, so a restart or an input
//--- change no longer wipes it
ledger.RebuildFromHistory();
Print
(ledger.Report(InpRewardRatio));
An EA can also be attached while one of its own positions is already open, which is normal after a restart. Adopting that position back takes a few more lines:
//--- adopt a position that was already open before this init
if
(
PositionSelect
(
_Symbol
))
  {
int
sid = ledger.SetupFromMagic(
PositionGetInteger
(
POSITION_MAGIC
));
if
(sid >=
0
)
      g_activePositionId =
PositionGetInteger
(
POSITION_IDENTIFIER
);
  }
Scoring the close
There are two ways to notice that a position has closed, and the two attached EAs use one each.
The demo EA uses OnTradeTransaction(), which fires the moment the deal lands:
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
//| TradeTransaction function                                        |
//| Score a position the moment it is fully closed.                  |
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
void
OnTradeTransaction
(
const
MqlTradeTransaction
&trans,
const
MqlTradeRequest
&request,
const
MqlTradeResult
&result)
  {
//--- only a closing deal of our own position is of interest
if
(trans.type !=
TRADE_TRANSACTION_DEAL_ADD
|| trans.deal ==
0
)
return
;
if
(!
HistoryDealSelect
(trans.deal))
return
;
long
entry =
HistoryDealGetInteger
(trans.deal,
DEAL_ENTRY
);
if
(entry !=
DEAL_ENTRY_OUT
&& entry !=
DEAL_ENTRY_OUT_BY
)
return
;
long
posId =
HistoryDealGetInteger
(trans.deal,
DEAL_POSITION_ID
);
if
(posId ==
0
|| posId != g_activePositionId)
return
;
//--- a partial close sends an OUT deal and leaves the position
//--- open. Wait until the position is gone, then score it once.
if
(IsPositionOpenById(posId))
return
