Run the cross-check before you believe any ledger, mine included. 74 + 57 = 131, the tester's trade count, and the tester's own long/short split is 74 and 57 as well. 150.65 - 148.53 = 2.12 USD, the tester's net profit to the cent. If those two lines do not reconcile, the ledger is miscounting positions somewhere and every percentage above them is worthless.
Three things come out of the split.
The two setups are not one trade and its mirror image. One made money, the other lost very nearly the same amount, and between them they canceled out. Their raw win rates are almost the same, 35.1% and 36.8%, so on win rate alone you would call them twins, and the bearish side would look like the slightly better of the two. It is the one that lost money. The difference is in how much each win paid against each loss, not in how often they won, and a win rate cannot show you that.
Both raw win rates clear the 33.3% that a nominal 1:2 ratio asks for, so on the input sheet both sides look profitable. Eight months of that produced 2.12 USD. The realized column is where the difference shows up. The bullish wins averaged 1.90 times the bullish losses, close to what the inputs asked for. The bearish wins averaged only 1.66 times, and that moves the bearish bar from 33.3% up to 37.6%, above its own 36.8% win rate. Against the input sheet the bearish setup clears its bar by three and a half points. Against the account it misses by nearly one. That is the gap between paper breakeven and account breakeven, measured instead of guessed. Why the bearish wins came out short, the ledger cannot tell you. Exits, spread and fills all end up in the same number.
And neither side gets a verdict beyond undecided. Both intervals are more than twenty points wide and both contain their bar: 25.2% to 46.5% against 34.5% on the bullish side, 25.5% to 49.8% against 37.6% on the bearish one. With that much room, the record cannot tell a setup that clears its bar from one that misses it, and it does not pretend to. The bullish side ended in profit, but that is not a verdict.
The other four runs agree. The wins and losses were identical in all five, 26/48 and 21/36, and only the fills moved: the bullish net landed between +131.32 and +174.22 USD, the bearish between -156.64 and -139.22, and the verdicts did not change.
