Reconcile it before reading anything into it. The counts are wins/losses, so the bullish setup traded 237 + 537 = 774 times and the bearish one 192 + 399 = 591, giving 1,365 trades against the tester's 1,365, and the same 774 long and 591 short the report lists. The two nets add to -6,838.22 USD, the tester's net loss to the cent.
Now the split says something. The bullish setup took 57% of the trades and produced 5,580.10 of the 6,838.22 loss, which is 82% of the damage. Across the five runs that share sat between 80.5% and 85.3%. The intervals are much narrower too, about six and a half points wide on the bullish side and seven and a half on the bearish, against more than twenty in the M15 run.
This is where the rejected verdict earns its place. Under the old two-way test both setups printed "not yet trusted", the same words the M15 run printed after 131 trades. Now they come apart. The bullish setup reads rejected: even the optimistic end of its interval, 34.0%, sits under the 34.7% its own wins and losses need. The bearish setup is still undecided. Its interval runs from 28.8% to 36.4% around a bar of 33.7%, so most of the range is below the line, but not all of it, and a net loss of 1,258.12 USD over 591 trades is not yet enough for the ledger to tell a slightly losing setup from a breakeven one. All five runs gave the same two verdicts.
How many trades would settle it? A rough answer comes from asking when the interval gets narrower than the gap it has to resolve. With a win rate p and a bar b, that takes about n = z^2
p
(1 - p) / (p - b)^2 trades. For the bearish setup, p = 32.5% and b = 33.7%, which comes to roughly 6,000 trades, ten times what it has. The count grows with the square of how close a setup sits to its bar, so a setup that is nearly breakeven can stay undecided for as long as you care to test it. The formula treats the bar as exact, and it is not, so read the answer as a floor.
The bullish margin is thin, though.34.0% against 34.7% is less than a point. Trades are also not independent (see Limitations), so the real interval is wider than the one printed.
I read the rejection as strong evidence against this setup in this test, not as proof.
There is a limit to what any of it tells you. Both setups were graded on one symbol over one period with one set of inputs, so the finding is about this backtest, not about bullish order blocks in general. What it does buy is direction. Without the split, M1 is just a bad result, and the natural reaction is to start retuning every input in the EA. With the split, the cheapest useful experiment is obvious: take the bullish entries out and run the same test again.
That shortens the list of things worth trying next, which is the job the ledger was built for.
The demo EA
SetupLedgerDemo.mq5 is there so you can watch the mechanism work on trades you can read through in an afternoon. Three unrelated setups, one position at a time, ordinary risk-based sizing.
Run it on any symbol with default inputs, "Every tick" with random delay and a 10,000 USD balance, over enough history to produce a couple of hundred trades. The final Report() block in the journal is the whole result, in the same shape as the scalper's block above, with three setup rows instead of two and your own numbers in them.
I have deliberately not printed my run of it here. The demo entries are a plain RSI bounce, an EMA cross and an N-bar breakout, chosen because they fire often and differ from each other, not because they make money. Whatever they earn on your symbol over your date range is a different number from whatever they earned on mine, and quoting mine would advertise a result you cannot reproduce.
Check two things against your own output instead. The three nets have to add up to the tester's net result exactly, the same reconciliation the scalper passed above; if they do not, the ledger is miscounting positions and nothing above that line means anything. And the three win rates will almost certainly not resemble each other, even though the aggregate report treats all three as one strategy.
